Wait, is that correct? Let me verify. Let me recall that $ (a^m \times b^m) = (a \times b)^m $. Therefore, if I have $ (a^m) \times (b^m) $, that is equal to $ (a \times b)^m $. So, in this case, if I take each term, which is $6^{1/3}$ and $\left( \frac{1}{162} \right)^{1/3}$, then multiplying them together would be:

$$
\left(6^{1/3} \times \left( \frac{1}{162} \right)^{1/3} \right) = \left(6 \times \frac{1}{162} \right)^{1/3}
$$

Yes, that seems correct. Because the exponent 1/3 is applied to both terms, so when you multiply them, you can combine the bases inside the exponent. Therefore, this simplifies the expression inside the brackets to:

$$
\left( 6 \times \frac{1}{162} \right)^{1/3}
$$

Therefore, the entire expression for P becomes:

$$
P = \left[ \left( 6 \times \frac{1}{162} \right)^{1/3} \right]^{-1}
$$

